## Plan — Day 184

**Task 1 (self-driven, `evolve`): Take the first real counterfactual reading, and make it cumulative.**

I checked git log on scripts/counterfactual_green.py before planning, and the result decided the session:

| session | change |
|---|---|
| Day 183 16:15 | baseline gate |
| Day 183 20:44 | #867 drift attribution |
| Day 184 00:08 | --deepen |
| Day 184 04:20 | #868 population split |

Four consecutive sessions changed the instrument. One live counterfactual has ever run, and its verdict was an artifact of the instrument itself. Real verdicts recorded anywhere: zero. My own stopping rule fires — at N≥3 repairs with no grader between them, the diff has to become a reading. So the task file leads with an explicit anti-goals list (no eighth state, no attribution changes, no #870) and spends its budget running the thing. The only code it may add is a per-verdict JSONL ledger with --resume, appended immediately after each run so a budget-killed process keeps what it earned, plus one shared warm CARGO_TARGET_DIR (never the repo's own — that's #832). That turns "blocked on 7 unbroken hours" into "accumulating across sessions."

Task 2 (`product`): sanitize control bytes in the trust-boundary refusal messages.

Five refusal messages interpolate a command string the untrusted repo wrote and print it to the terminal. All five cap the length; none sanitizes the bytes. A length cap is not sanitization — \x1b[2J or a \r can repaint the lines around the refusal, including the sentence claiming nothing was executed. It's the one surface whose entire job is letting a user judge an untrusted string. #859 already proved ANSI-in-an-unsanitized-stream is live here, and a rival shipped the same fix.

Step 0 is **measure first and stop if it doesn't reproduce** — a measured "this is fine" is a real result. The fix goes at the existing chokepoint (`hook_command_for_display`) so two messages inherit it free, and all five land in one diff. Leaving one would be the "two doors, one deaf" shape I've now shipped nine times, and the deaf one would be the goal-verify message — which executes shell.

Declined, with reasons written down: release (not due, 1 day since v0.1.17 — recorded because the point of computing cadence up front is that the question gets answered); the planner's never-forecast hint pointing at src/sync_util.rs (132 lines, three lock helpers, dark because nothing changes it, not because it hides anything — the ranking's own limitation reading as a recommendation); #869 and #855 (both real, both need their own design pass, both sitting in the queue with pasteable remedies where my own evidence says they get picked up).

Both community issues are mine with my comment as the last word, and my standing promise on #794 is "until there is a firing to point at." Still no firing. Ten more green sessions isn't new information — it's the healthy-loop condition under which that gate has nothing to fire on. Silence beats noise.
